  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Sunday)\nIssued at 1232 PM MDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nLatest water vapor imagery shows 500hpa low spinning across \nsouthern WA state with ridging setting up from eastern MT into \neastern CO. Upper flow across the Dakotas has turned more \nnorthwesterly with broad cyclonic flow setting up across the Great\nLakes into New England. Broad high pressure still remains in \ncontrol across the southern tier of states. At the surface, low \npressure is setting up across southern WY. Southeast surface winds\nahead of this low are advecting in a lower cloud deck out of NE \ninto much of the forecast area. Temperatures at 18Z are in the low\nto mid 60s. \n\nThe forecast through the next 24 hours presents some interesting \nchallenges. First in a series of shortwaves ejects into the FA \nthis evening, with strengthening 700hpa theta-e forcing and \nmoisture advection, as seen with increasing 850hpa dewpoints. This\nshould lead to increasing coverage of rainfall across the \nforecast area beginning later this evening and continuing through \nthe night. Can't rule out some elevated convection. Hi-res models \nare showing the potential for some of these storms to mix down the\nstrong 700hpa and 850hpa winds as well. The rain will be welcome \nfor sure. On Monday, the rain will push east of the FA during the \nmorning hours. The cold front sweeps through with gusty NW winds \nfound on the backside along with modest CAA. By 18Z Monday models\nare showing from 4 to 5 mb/3hrly pressure rises on backside of \nfront. It appears that winds will become nearly linked during the \nafternoon hours, so have opted to hoist a wind advisory for parts \nof our northern FA during the day Monday. This also leads to a \ntricky fire weather concern. Parts of our NW are at critical fire \ndanger levels. The unknown is how much rain they may receive \novernight, helping to put a small damper on those critical levels.\nAdditionally, there might only be a narrow window when RH values \nand winds meet up for fire weather headlines. For now, am going to\nforego any fire weather headlines and let later shifts evaluate. \nAlso, given CAA and anticipated cloud cover, lowered high \ntemperatures on Monday. \n\nAfter Monday, the rest of the week still appears to be seasonable\ntemperature wise. Precipitation chances move back into the area\nbeginning late Tuesday night and generally continue through the\nrest of the week.",
